title: Writes and atomicity
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: One write is one transaction — rows, log, HEAD and every derived key space — and the map grows at the start of each write.

!! One write, one transaction

One `log::Writer` write is one write transaction of the store, and it holds
everything the write changes:

* the field rows;
* the log's operations and snapshots, and HEAD;
* ''every'' index (derived key space).

It commits entirely or not at all. So the indexes are consistent with HEAD by
construction: there is no snapshot-plus-replay scheme, and nothing on the read path
"catches up" to HEAD.

Bulk writers (reconcile, a large watcher flush) accumulate their index updates in
memory, a chunk at a time, and write each chunk once. There is one writer at a time:
the executor and the API already serialise writes.

!! The map, and how large a write can be

LMDB reserves the whole ''map'' — the address range the file is mapped into — when
it opens, and can enlarge it only while the process has no transaction open. A write
transaction can therefore never write more than the room left in the map when it
started (the map's size minus what the store already uses). Too large a write fails
whole with `MDB_MAP_FULL`, without corruption.

So, just before each write transaction (`KvStore::grow_map`, `map_to_grant`), the
daemon gives it room:

* the room granted is the ''free space of the disk'', capped at 128 GiB per store;
* it is renewed only when it has fallen below what must be guaranteed — all of the free space on a disk with less than 128 GiB free (store and free space shrink together, so this is not a resize at every write), 64 GiB otherwise.

Consequences:

* a write of any size — the first reconcile of a large tree — commits, unless the disk is full or a single write exceeds 64 GiB;
* the address space holds the maps of some 900 loaded repositories (a process has 128 TiB of it);
* where the free space cannot be read, the room is at least what the store already holds.

Two earlier rules failed: a fixed 1 TiB map ran out of address space at about 120
repositories, and doubling the map when half full failed any write larger than the
store already was.
